# Nino - minimal Apache hardening defaults. Harmless if AllowOverride
# doesn't apply this file (eg. a PHP-FPM/nginx deployment) - see
# docs/deployment.md's "Nginx and Other Web Servers" for the equivalent rules
# there.

# Never serve a dotfile directly - matches the filename only (not the
# containing directory, so _editor/.cache/script.js is unaffected).
# private/.auth/ and private/.logs/ are dotdirs whose files inside
# (eg. 2026-01-01.php) aren't themselves dotfiles - the complete private tree
# is denied by private/.htaccess, with self-exiting .php stubs as an additional
# safeguard (see docs/development.md's "Outputs and Uploads", and the
# go-live checklist in docs/deployment.md).
<FilesMatch "^\.">
	Require all denied
</FilesMatch>

# ...and the contents of a dot *directory*, which the rule above cannot see:
# it matches filenames, so a checkout's .git/config reads as a plain "config"
# to it and is served - local history, remotes and hooks with it.
# docs/getting-started.md installs by cloning into the webroot, so that is the
# normal case rather than an exotic one, and docs/deployment.md already
# requires the equivalent for nginx.
# Conditioned on the request actually resolving to something on disk, which is
# the same line router.php draws on the dev server: dot-uris that are routes
# rather than files (/.form, /.newsletter, /.demo-catalogue) never touch the
# filesystem and have to keep falling through to index.php.
# .cache/ (the generated css+js bundles) and .demo/ (the demo images) are
# served deliberately - the two exceptions router.php makes as well.
# .well-known/ is a third one here: certificate renewal reads it on a
# production host, which is the one environment this file applies to.
<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
	RewriteEngine On
	RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} -f [OR]
	RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} -d
	RewriteRule "(^|/)\.(?!cache/|demo/|well-known/)" - [F]
</IfModule>

# The project's private half (see \Nino\Filesystem::PRIVATE_DIRS) is only ever
# read by php, never served - the stylesheet and script sources under
# private/assets/ included, since nothing requests one of those either: they
# are read off disk and concatenated into public/.cache/. Denied by
# private/.htaccess rather than from here, since a rule written here would have
# to name an absolute url path and would therefore miss a subdirectory install
# (/nino/dir). Both are belt and braces next to the 403 stub the php files in
# there carry themselves - the templates and the asset sources are not php and
# have only the two server rules.
#
# Its mirror, public/, is the opposite: everything in there is meant to be
# served, and nothing else in this project root is - index.php and the tool
# folders aside, a request that resolves to a real file outside public/ is
# almost always a mistake. The installer's appearance catalogue is the one
# place inside a tool folder with a deliberately public file in it, so
# _install/library/.htaccess denies everything there except the theme
# picker images.

# Don't let a directory with no index.php list its contents - a plain
# defence-in-depth default now that nothing relies on an unguessable
# directory name any more
Options -Indexes
